{
  "id": "11-close-the-door",
  "title": "Close the Door, Begin Again",
  "tenet": "Repent, repair, and guard the way forward",
  "durationMs": 72960,
  "audioAsset": "kingdom/11-close-the-door.mp3",
  "transcriptAsset": "assets/kingdom/11-close-the-door.json",
  "text": "Which door keeps opening after you have asked God to close it?\n\nA man prayed each morning to leave a destructive habit behind. Each evening he walked the same route, passed the same doorway, and told himself he would only look inside. When he fell again, he thought repentance meant saying better words the next morning. An older friend listened, then walked home with him by a different street. Together they removed the easy access and planned whom he would call when the urge returned. The man still needed mercy. He also needed a new path.\n\nTrue repentance is neither denial nor a life of shame. Confess the wrong; God is faithful to forgive and cleanse. Make repair where you can. Learn the hour, place, feeling, or excuse that leads you back, and seal that door with a practical boundary. Ask for support when the pattern is hard to control; some struggles need skilled care as well as prayer. Do not leave the cleared space empty. Return to the goals you have offered God. Fill your mind each day with Scripture, prayer, honest work, and service. If you stumble, come into the light again. Mercy is not exhausted, and a guarded path can be walked one faithful step at a time.",
